  ],
};

const safetyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Electricity\'s three killers', note: 'Shock (current through the body), arc flash (explosive plasma), and fire (overheated conductors). The trade\'s safety rules map to each.' },
    { math: '2 · Lockout, test, prove', note: 'De-energise, lock the breaker, test with a meter, prove the meter on a known source. Every time — the rule that keeps electricians alive.' },
    { math: '3 · Ohm\'s law runs everything', note: 'V = IR and P = VI: the maths behind wire sizing, breaker ratings and why resistance makes heat.' },
    { math: '4 · Voltage drop is a code issue', note: 'Long, thin wires waste voltage as heat. Codes limit drop (typically ~3–5%) because motors and lights starve at the far end.' },
    { math: '5 · Protection finishes the circuit', note: 'Breakers and fuses sized to the conductor: the overcurrent device is what turns a fault into a tripped switch instead of a fire.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/electrical-trades/foundational/electrical-safety-and-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The electrical trade runs on two texts: the safety procedure that keeps its workers alive
    and the circuit theory that makes its calculations work. Everything else — wire tables,
    breaker sizing, code articles — is arithmetic between those two covers, and the
    foundational unit teaches them together on purpose. The theory tells you what the current
    is doing; the safety procedure tells you what the current will do to you. An electrician
    who knows one without the other is either a hazard or a bookkeeper.
  </p>
  <p>
    It helps to start from what electricity in a building actually is: energy moving through
    conductors at (nearly) the speed of light, invisible, silent, and instantaneous in its
    consequences. Unlike most trades' materials, wire does not give warnings — a conductor at
    a lethal voltage looks identical to a dead one, which is why the trade's most important
    rule is a <em>procedure</em> rather than a perception: never trust appearances, always
    test. That rule, and the physics that justifies every other one, is this page's content.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three pillars below map to the unit's syllabus: Ohm's law and circuit behaviour (the
    theory), test instruments (the tools that make electricity visible), and the safe-work
    standards — OSHA's general duty framework and NFPA 70E's arc-flash procedures — that turn
    theory into a survivable career. Then a bridge section shows where the physics meets the
    codebook, because that is where the trade actually lives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the killers, the procedure, the law, the protection" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />

  <h2>Ohm's law and circuits</h2>
  <p>
    Circuit theory rests on three quantities and one law. <strong>Voltage</strong> (V, in
    volts) is the electrical pressure pushing charge; <strong>current</strong> (I, in amps) is
    the flow rate of that charge; <strong>resistance</strong> (R, in ohms, Ω) is the opposition
    to flow. Ohm's law binds them — V = I × R — and its sibling, the power law P = V × I,
    converts flow into heat and work. Run a worked example: a 2,300-watt kettle on a 230 V
    supply draws 2,300 ÷ 230 = 10 A, and its heating element therefore has a resistance of
    230 ÷ 10 = 23 Ω. Every calculation in the trade — element sizing, breaker selection,
    transformer loading — is this arithmetic at scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    Circuits come in two arrangements whose behaviour every electrician must see instantly.
    <strong>Series</strong>: one path, current identical everywhere, resistances add, and one
    open breaks everything (old Christmas lights). <strong>Parallel</strong>: multiple paths,
    voltage identical across each branch, currents add — and building wiring is parallel for
    exactly this reason, so every outlet sees full voltage and switching off the kettle does
    not darken the kitchen. A subtlety with enormous practical weight: <strong>resistance
    makes heat</strong> (P = I²R — current squared!). Double the current through a poor
    connection and the heat there quadruples — which is why loose terminals smoulder, why
    aluminium wiring's problems were connection problems, and why every ampacity table in the
    codebook exists to keep conductor heat inside its insulation's tolerance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> voltage kills. Current through the body is what kills —
    roughly 30–50 mA across the chest can trigger fatal ventricular fibrillation — but Ohm's
    law links them: higher voltage drives more current through the body's resistance (around
    1,000 Ω wet skin, tens of thousands dry). The practical reading: it is current you must
    keep out of your body, and wet conditions slash the resistance that protects you.
  </p>

  <h2>Test instruments</h2>
  <p>
    The meter never lies — but only if you know which meter, set to which function, on which
    range. The <strong>multimeter</strong> is the trade's knife: volts AC and DC, amps,
    ohms/continuity. Its uses are drilled as pairs: voltage <em>across</em> a component
    (parallel), current <em>through</em> the circuit (in series, or more safely via a clamp),
    resistance only on a de-energised circuit (measuring resistance on a live circuit damages
    the meter and lies). The <strong>clamp meter</strong> reads current inductively around a
    single conductor — no breaking the circuit — and is the daily tool for load checks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three more instruments carry the safety weight. The <strong>two-pole voltage tester</strong>
    (or approved proximity tester) is what you test a circuit with before touching it — simple,
    robust, hard to mislead. The <strong>proving unit</strong> is the discipline's honesty
    device: before you believe a "dead" reading, prove the tester itself on a known live
    source — the "test, then prove the test" habit that catches the dead-battery-in-the-meter
    failure which has killed experienced electricians. The <strong>insulation resistance
    tester</strong> (megger) applies hundreds of volts to check insulation integrity on
    installations and motors. And the meters themselves carry safety ratings — CAT II, III, IV —
    that specify the fault-energy environment they survive; a cheap CAT II meter on a service
    panel is itself an arc-flash hazard. Modern practice adds the thermal camera, which reads
    the I²R heating this section is all about — hot spots on connections are resistance made
    visible.
  </p>

  <h2>OSHA and NFPA 70E safe work</h2>
  <p>
    Electricity has three killers, and the safety standards map to each.
    <strong>Shock</strong> — current through the body — is defeated by de-energising (the
    only real protection), lockout/tagout (locks on the disconnect so nobody re-energises
    while your hands are in the panel), and the test-and-prove discipline above.
    <strong>Arc flash</strong> is the hazard shock rules do not touch: a short circuit across
    air that vaporises conductor metal in an explosive plasma ball, with temperatures near
    19,000 °C, copper spray, a pressure blast and a light flash — survivable only by
    distance and PPE. NFPA 70E, the arc-flash standard, requires hazard analysis, labelled
    incident-energy levels, approach boundaries (how close you may stand), and arc-rated
    clothing and face protection matched to the energy.
    <strong>Fire</strong> — the long fuse — is defeated by the code itself: conductors and
    overcurrent devices sized so nothing ever reaches ignition temperature in normal service.
  </p>
  <p>
    The procedure that condenses all of it is drilled until reflex: <strong>de-energise,
    lock, tag, test, prove</strong> — then, and only then, touch. These rules descend from
    memorials: arcs that vaporised copper at 19,000 °C, panels re-energised by someone's
    "just a second". OSHA's regulations give the legal backbone (employers must provide a
    workplace free of recognised hazards; electrical work practices are codified), NFPA 70E
    gives the method, and the apprentice's own habits give the margin. Electricity gives no
    second chance at belief.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> low voltage means safe, and rubber-soled shoes make you
    immune. Household 230 V kills routinely because skin's wet resistance drops enough for
    lethal current; and consumer footwear is not rated electrical PPE — insulation comes
    from mats, gloves rated and tested for the voltage class, and distance. The safe
    assumption is always: energised until proven dead.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="voltage drop vs current, length and wire size" widget="sliderLab" config={dropCfg} />

  <h2>From theory to the codebook</h2>
  <p>
    Every conductor resists, so every run loses voltage: drop = I × R, and R grows with
    length and shrinks with cross-section (copper's resistivity ≈ 0.0175 Ω·mm² per metre).
    Run the widget's numbers: a 20 A load 100 m away on 4 mm² wire loses about 17.5 V — over
    7 per cent of a 230 V supply, enough to brown out lights and stall motors (motors that
    run hot on low voltage draw <em>more</em> current, heating themselves further — a
    feedback loop the code's 3–5 per cent drop guidance exists to prevent). Bigger wire is
    the fix, and "how big?" is a codebook lookup built on this lesson's arithmetic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Protection completes every circuit. <strong>Overcurrent devices</strong> — breakers and
    fuses — are sized to the conductor, not the load, so that a fault anywhere trips in
    milliseconds instead of heating the wiring toward fire; the logic is I²R all the way
    down. <strong>Grounding and bonding</strong> give fault current a low-resistance path
    home so the breaker sees the fault and clears it, and hold every metal part at earth
    potential so a person touching it does not become the path. Modern devices add
    intelligence to the same physics: <strong>GFCI/RCD</strong> protection compares current
    out against current back and trips on the few milliamps of imbalance that mean "through
    a person"; <strong>AFCI</strong> protection recognises the signature of arcing faults in
    wiring. The National Electrical Code is the trade's operating manual, organised by
    installation type and full of tables for ampacity, fill and grounding — but its logic is
    this lesson's physics: conductors sized so their insulation never cooks, protection
    sized so faults clear instantly, grounding so faults have a safe path home.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked circuit: one switch, one light</h2>
  <p>
    The theory becomes concrete in the smallest real installation: a lighting circuit. The
    supply (a 20 A breaker on the panel) feeds a cable to the switch and on to the light
    fitting; the switch interrupts only the <em>line</em> (hot) conductor, never the
    neutral — so that an off switch leaves the fitting with no voltage on it, which is the
    electrician's reason for the wiring convention. Size the parts: a 60 W lamp on 230 V
    draws 60 ÷ 230 ≈ 0.26 A — trivially inside a 20 A breaker, because the breaker's job is
    not to track the lamp but to protect the 1.5 mm² cable, whose ampacity (roughly 15–20 A
    depending on installation method) sets the limit. Now trace the fault-finding the
    apprentice learns on this circuit: switch on, lamp dark — meter across the fitting
    reads 230 V (supply fine, lamp open-circuit: burnt bulb) versus reads zero at the
    fitting but full voltage at the switch (open between them: loose terminal or broken
    conductor). Every reading is Ohm's law and series logic applied — voltage present where
    the circuit is complete, voltage appearing across the break. The whole trade is this
    circuit, scaled and multiplied: more switches, more lights, bigger cables, three
    phases — same three quantities, same one law, same habit of measuring before believing.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>AC adds its own subtleties.</strong> Alternating current's reversals give
    transformers their magic (voltage up, current down, transmission losses slashed — the
    reason grids are AC) but add impedance: inductors and capacitors oppose current in ways
    that depend on frequency, and power factor measures how much of the current actually
    does work. Three-phase supply — the commercial standard — delivers smoother power and
    simpler motors; single-phase is the residential norm. The apprentice meets these as
    "beyond the basics"; the electrician eventually lives in them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Arc-flash analysis is quantitative engineering.</strong> Incident energy (cal/cm²)
    is computed from available fault current, clearing time and working distance — which is
    why breaker settings matter for safety as well as coordination, and why the label on the
    panel is the summary of a study, not a decoration. The profession's safety frontier keeps
    advancing the same direction: eliminate the hazard first (work de-energised as the
    default, energised work as documented exception), engineer the exposure second, dress
    for the arc last. It is the same hierarchy every trade learns, stated in the only
    language this trade's hazard speaks: volts, amps, ohms — and the arithmetic between
    them.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCCER Electrical curriculum; NFPA 70 (NEC) and NFPA 70E; Delmar's <em>Standard Textbook of Electricity</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
